## Architecture

The application follows a layered backend architecture:

```text
Client / PHP Frontend
        │
        │ HTTPS
        ▼
   Node.js + Express
        │
        ├── Routes
        │
        ├── Controllers
        │
        ├── Services
        │
        ├── Validators
        │
        ├── Middleware
        │
        └── Models
        │
        ▼
     MongoDB
```

AI document processing will eventually use an asynchronous workflow:

```text
PDF / Image
     │
     ▼
Document Storage
     │
     ▼
AI Processing
     │
     ▼
Extracted Data
     │
     ▼
Validation
     │
     ▼
Transaction Draft
     │
     ▼
User Confirmation
     │
     ▼
Transaction
```

AI-generated information will **never be treated as automatically trusted financial data**.

---

## Multi-Tenant Architecture

The application is designed as a multi-tenant SaaS.

A business owns its financial data:

```text
Business
│
├── Users
├── Categories
├── Transactions
├── Documents
├── Customers
├── Suppliers
└── Reports
```

Business-owned resources will contain a `businessId`.

Example:

```js
{
    businessId: "...",
    type: "expense",
    amount: 15000,
    categoryId: "...",
    date: "2026-08-08"
}
```

The authenticated user's `businessId` will be used to scope database queries.

The client will not be trusted to provide or modify the tenant identity.

---

## Security Principles

Security is a core part of the architecture.

The backend will follow these principles:

1. Never trust client-provided tenant IDs.
2. Validate every API request.
3. Verify authorization on every protected resource.
4. Never expose MongoDB directly to the internet.
5. Never store plaintext passwords.
6. Never store secrets in source control.
7. Never trust uploaded files.
8. Never trust AI-generated financial data without validation.
9. Keep sensitive documents in private storage.
10. Maintain audit logs for important financial actions.
11. Apply rate limiting to sensitive endpoints.
12. Avoid permanently deleting important financial records where an audit-friendly alternative is appropriate.

---

## Project Structure

The planned backend structure:

```text
src/
│
├── config/
│   ├── database.js
│   └── env.js
│
├── controllers/
│
├── middleware/
│
├── models/
│
├── routes/
│
├── services/
│
├── validators/
│
├── utils/
│
├── app.js
└── server.js
```

The main request flow will follow:

```text
Route
  ↓
Middleware
  ↓
Controller
  ↓
Service
  ↓
Model
  ↓
MongoDB
```

Business logic should primarily live inside services rather than becoming tightly coupled to controllers.

---

## Environment Variables

Create a `.env` file locally.

Example:

```env
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=5000

MONGO_URI=

JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

CLIENT_URL=
```

Never commit the real `.env` file to Git.

Use `.env.example` to document required environment variables.

---

## Installation

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
```

Navigate into the project:

```bash
cd finance-saas-backend
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Create your environment file:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
```

Configure the required environment variables.

Start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

---

## Development

The project uses `nodemon` for development.

Example `package.json` scripts:

```json
{
    "scripts": {
        "dev": "nodemon src/server.js",
        "start": "node src/server.js"
    }
}
```

---

## API Versioning

The API will use versioning from the beginning:

```text
/api/v1
```

Example:

```text
/api/v1/auth/register
/api/v1/auth/login
/api/v1/transactions
/api/v1/categories
```

This allows future API versions to be introduced without immediately breaking existing clients.

